Option B: Prompt Lookup Decoding (The Universal Solution)
If you want zero-setup, zero-VRAM, 100% universal speculation that works on any model without downloading a second model, you should implement Prompt Lookup Decoding (PLD).

How it works:
Instead of using a neural network to guess tokens, PLD uses simple string matching (n-gram lookups) directly on the prompt and generated context:

When generating, the engine looks at the last $N$ tokens generated (e.g., the last 3 tokens: def, __init__, (self).
It searches the input prompt (which contains your codebase, documentation, or conversation history) for the exact same 3-token sequence.
If it finds a match, it reads the next $K$ tokens that followed that sequence in the prompt and uses them as the "draft" tokens.
The verifier (Vaayu) verifies these draft tokens in a single forward pass.
Why it fits a general-purpose coding engine:
Zero VRAM / Zero Weights: It uses a fast CPU/numpy string search. VRAM footprint is $0$ MB.
100% Tokenizer/Model Agnostic: It works on any tokenizer, any custom model, and any custom architecture out of the box.
Highly Effective for Coding: Code contains massive amounts of repeating boilerplate, imports, and variable names. If a function signature exists in the prompt, PLD will guess it with $100%$ accuracy when writing the implementation.


